<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What leaves the device through Android's backup service.

    These are allowlists. Naming even one <include> stops everything else being backed up,
    so anything the app stores in future is outside the backup set until someone deliberately
    puts it here. That is the point: the Navigation API key lives in shared preferences, and a
    rule that worked by listing exclusions would leak it the day somebody forgot one. Listing
    the sensitive files as exclusions as well is not available as a safety net — lint rejects
    an exclude that is not inside an included path, precisely because the include already
    excluded it.

    Two things go:

      backup/rides.backup  ride summaries, written by RideBackupStore. Not the database —
                           that carries the telemetry sample series, which is around half a
                           megabyte for every hour ridden against a 25 MB allowance for the
                           whole app, so it would blow the budget in a couple of days and
                           then stop backing anything up at all.
      app_settings.xml     the rider's preferences. Nothing secret, nothing tied to a device.

    Everything else stays behind, and the two kinds that matter are worth naming.
    secure_navigation_settings holds the Navigation API key. bike_identity, associated_bike,
    ble_protection_trust, bike_connection_demand and connection_diagnostics each describe one
    bond between this phone and one motorcycle, so restoring them onto a different phone would
    describe a pairing that does not exist.

    disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities keeps ride history, which is location data, off any
    device that cannot encrypt the backup with the rider's own lock screen.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true">
        <include domain="file" path="backup/rides.backup" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="app_settings.xml" />
    </cloud-backup>

    <!--
        Device-to-device transfer has no 25 MB limit, but it restores through the same code
        path as a cloud restore and gains nothing from diverging. Keeping the two identical
        means there is one thing to reason about rather than two.
    -->
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="backup/rides.backup" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="app_settings.xml" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
